// INST1 Academy question bank, units 1 to 3. All questions are original jobsite scenarios.
window.FRA = window.FRA || {};
FRA.questions = FRA.questions || [];
FRA.questions.push(
// u1l1
{id:"u1l1-1",t:"u1l1",q:"A new HVAC duct hangs exactly where the drawing routes your cable. What is the FIRST thing to do?",a:["Hang the cable from the duct hangers since they are close and already in place","Stop work on that route and tell the foreman so the designer can settle it","Route the cable under the duct and note the change on your own copy later","Move the duct a short way so that your pathway matches the drawing exactly"],c:1,e:"Conflicts between trades go up the chain to the foreman and the designer. Hanging from the duct, moving it, or working around it all touch another trade's work or break the plan."},
{id:"u1l1-2",t:"u1l1",q:"You need to move a device that sits on the fire alarm circuit above the ceiling you are working in. Who owns that work?",a:["The electrician who is hired for the panel upgrades on the same floor","The HVAC contractor, because devices and ducts share the same ceiling space, and then move on to the next task","The fire alarm contractor, so you ask the foreman to have that trade move it","The general contractor's site cleanup crew, who handle loose ceiling items, which saves time on the job"],c:2,e:"Each trade owns its own work. You never move or cover another trade's devices; the foreman arranges it with the trade that owns the fire alarm system."},
{id:"u1l1-3",t:"u1l1",q:"At the end of the shift, cable scraps, packaging, and dust lie in the corridor. What is the expected practice?",a:["Leave it for the general contractor's cleanup crew at the end of the whole project","Stack it neatly in a corner of the corridor so it is easy to find the next shift","Sweep only the areas the owner is most likely to walk through the next day","Clean up daily before leaving"],c:3,e:"Daily cleanup is part of professional conduct. Debris left overnight is a trip hazard, a fire load, and a sign of careless work."},
{id:"u1l1-4",t:"u1l1",q:"A helper who has just passed Installer 1 wants to specialize in splicing and terminating fiber. Which certificate comes next?",a:["Installer 2 Optical Fiber, which builds on the Installer 1 skills with fiber work","RCDD, which is the first step on the installation pathway after Installer 1","A second Installer 1 certificate, which must be renewed at regular intervals, as is the usual habit on many sites","Installer 2 Copper, which covers copper termination and testing in more depth, which many installers prefer to do"],c:0,e:"Installer 1 is nonrenewable and leads to Installer 2 Copper or Installer 2 Optical Fiber, then the Technician level. The RCDD belongs to the later design path."},
{id:"u1l1-5",t:"u1l1",q:"During a pull you notice a section of cable was kinked, and the foreman is busy on another floor. What is the BEST action?",a:["Pull the rest of the cable through and test it at the end to see if it passes, which many installers prefer to do","Report the damaged cable to the foreman now and replace it rather than leaving it in the wall","Mark the spot and cut it out after the inspector has finished the walk-through, a shortcut that crews often take","Leave the kink inside the wall and hide it behind the faceplate for now"],c:1,e:"A kinked cable is damaged and must be replaced. Reporting problems early is part of honest, workmanlike conduct, and it costs far less than finding the defect at inspection."},

// u1l2
{id:"u1l2-1",t:"u1l2",q:"You are about to start a task in an area you have never worked. What should you do FIRST?",a:["Start the task and correct hazards as they appear while you work","Begin with the easiest part of the task to learn what the space is like","Review the site safety plan and the hazards","Ask the nearest worker which hazards were worst during the last month"],c:2,e:"The safety plan and a job hazard review come before the task. Each hazard gets a control before work starts, not after the near miss."},
{id:"u1l2-2",t:"u1l2",q:"The foreman checks how you set an extension ladder against a wall. Which pitch rule is he looking for?",a:["Base out 1 unit for every 2 units of height so the ladder is steep","Base out 1 unit for every 8 units of height so that it stands nearly upright","Base directly against the wall, with the top tied off to a ceiling grid wire","Base out 1 unit for every 4 units of working height of the ladder"],c:3,e:"The 4-to-1 rule puts the base 1 unit out for every 4 units of working height. Too steep tips backward and too shallow lets the base slide out."},
{id:"u1l2-3",t:"u1l2",q:"You will step off an extension ladder onto a landing. What is the BEST way to set the top of the ladder?",a:["Extend it 1 m (3.28 ft) above the landing so you can step on and off safely","Stop it level with the landing so the rails do not catch your tool belt, on the theory that it is good enough","Place it so that the top rung is even with the landing edge for balance","Tie two shorter ladders together so the top reaches well past the landing, since that is quicker for the whole crew"],c:0,e:"The ladder top extends about 1 m (3.28 ft) above a landing, which gives something to hold when stepping on and off. Never join two ladders unless they were built to be joined."},
{id:"u1l2-4",t:"u1l2",q:"A panel near your work has no label and the foreman cannot be reached. May you assume it is de-energized?",a:["Yes, because an unlabeled panel is normally not connected to any service, as most crews do on a busy day","No, treat it as live; only the person who controls the circuit locks and tags it","Yes, if the breaker handles are all in the off position when you look","No, unless a coworker tells you that he checked it with his meter earlier, since that is quicker for the whole crew"],c:1,e:"Installers never work on energized power. Lockout and tagout is done by the person who controls the circuit, and unknown wiring is treated as live."},
{id:"u1l2-5",t:"u1l2",q:"Coring a concrete slab throws up fine gray dust. What is the BEST control?",a:["Open a window and let the dust settle on the floor before you sweep","Wear a paper mask, since the dust cannot reach your lungs through it","Use water or a vacuum at the cutting point and wear a respirator, because the dust contains silica","Move the crew to another floor while the dust settles after each core"],c:2,e:"Drilling and coring concrete makes silica dust. Water or a vacuum controls it at the source, and a respirator protects the person near the cut."},

// u1l3
{id:"u1l3-1",t:"u1l3",q:"The ceiling above a corridor is an air return. Which jacket print shows a cable is acceptable there?",a:["General purpose rating printed on the jacket, matching the category the job needs","Limited use rating printed on the jacket, which suits short runs in raceways","Riser rating printed on the jacket, since it covers both floors and ceilings","Plenum rating (CMP) printed on the jacket, matching the category the job needs"],c:3,e:"An air-handling ceiling is a plenum, and cable there must carry a plenum rating (CMP). A lower rating, even a good cable, is a violation in that space."},
{id:"u1l3-2",t:"u1l3",q:"A designer needs a cable rated to 250 MHz for the new drops. Which cable fits that rating?",a:["Category 6, which often has a spline or a tighter twist to control crosstalk","Category 5e, which is the minimum for new work and has a 100 MHz rating, on the theory that it is good enough","Category 6A, which is the augmented type with a rating of 500 MHz","Category 3, which is the older voice grade cable with a short run length, as most crews do on a busy day"],c:0,e:"Category 6 is rated to 250 MHz. Category 5e is 100 MHz and Category 6A is 500 MHz, and Category 6A also has a larger outside diameter."},
{id:"u1l3-3",t:"u1l3",q:"Why do patch cords use stranded conductors when permanent horizontal runs use solid ones?",a:["Stranded carries more signal than solid, so cords can be any length at all","Stranded flexes without breaking but loses more signal, so cords stay short","Solid conductors cannot be punched down, so cords use stranded wire only","Solid conductors are banned in cords by code, though they carry the signal better"],c:1,e:"Stranded wire flexes without breaking but loses more signal, so cords stay short. Solid wire suits permanent runs and punches down onto an IDC."},
{id:"u1l3-4",t:"u1l3",q:"A voice backbone uses large multipair cable. Which describes how its pairs are grouped?",a:["Four pairs in a foil wrap with a drain wire on each pair","Pairs stranded together with a metal center spine and no color code, and then move on to the next task","Bundled pairs in 25-pair binder groups, each group marked by a colored binder","Twenty-four pairs per group so that each group matches one fiber strand, on the theory that it is good enough"],c:2,e:"Multipair backbone cable is built in 25-pair binder groups, and a colored binder identifies each group in a large cable."},
{id:"u1l3-5",t:"u1l3",q:"Which coax cable is the standard 75 ohm choice for new video and cable television runs?",a:["RG-59, which is the thickest and has the lowest loss of the three common types","RG-11, which is made only for short runs of camera cable inside a rack","RG-58, which is a 75 ohm cable and the usual choice for radio systems","RG-6, standard for new video"],c:3,e:"RG-6 is the standard 75 ohm cable for video and cable television. RG-59 is older and thinner with more loss, and RG-11 is thick, low-loss feeder cable."},

// u1l4
{id:"u1l4-1",t:"u1l4",q:"A reel arrives with an orange jacket, but the drawing calls for aqua fiber. What is the BEST way to find out what the fiber really is?",a:["Read the type printed on the jacket, because jacket color is a convention and not a rule","Return the whole reel because aqua is the only color OM3 and OM4 may use","Call the manufacturer and wait for an answer before anyone touches the reel","Assume orange means OM1 and keep it until a tester shows a different loss"],c:0,e:"Jacket color is voluntary and varies by maker. The print on the jacket gives the true type, so read it before judging the reel."},
{id:"u1l4-2",t:"u1l4",q:"Which fiber type has a core of about 9 micron?",a:["OM3 multimode, laser optimized, used for short links in the building","Singlemode (OS1 or OS2)","OM5 wideband multimode in lime green, used for short wideband links","OM1 multimode, with a 62.5 micron core and an orange jacket"],c:1,e:"Singlemode fiber has a core of about 9 micron and usually a yellow jacket. All multimode fibers have a larger core, 50 or 62.5 micron."},
{id:"u1l4-3",t:"u1l4",q:"A cable must run outdoors and then enter the building. Which construction is made for the outdoor stretch and must be transitioned inside?",a:["Tight buffer, with a 900 micron coating on each fiber, used inside buildings","Plenum jacket, with a flame-retardant coating on tight buffered fibers","Loose tube, with 250 micron fibers floating in a gel or dry tube, transitioned at the entrance","Interlocking armor, which makes the cable safe to run anywhere in the building"],c:2,e:"Loose-tube cable protects fibers from water and suits outdoor runs, but it must be transitioned to inside cable at the building entry. Tight buffer is the inside style."},
{id:"u1l4-4",t:"u1l4",q:"In a 24-fiber cable you are asked to terminate strand 14. What color is it?",a:["Orange with no tracer, because the colors only repeat after strand 24","Blue with a black tracer, since it is the first color of the second group","Brown with a yellow tracer, since every strand in the second group is yellow","Orange with a black tracer, the second color in the second group of twelve"],c:3,e:"Strands 13 to 24 repeat the 12-color code with a black tracer. Strand 14 is the second color, orange, so it is orange with a black tracer."},
{id:"u1l4-5",t:"u1l4",q:"An armored fiber cable with interlocking armor enters the TR. What must be done with the armor?",a:["Bond it to the telecom bonding system, because it is a metal path","Tie it to the rack frame with cable ties so it cannot move","Cut it back to the jacket and leave it unterminated to avoid shorts","Ground it at the far end only, where the cable leaves the outdoor trench"],c:0,e:"Interlocking armor is metal, so it is bonded to the telecom bonding system. Plain jackets need no bonding."},

// u1l5
{id:"u1l5-1",t:"u1l5",q:"The foreman unrolls a floor plan. Which part of the sheet tells you whether you hold the current drawing?",a:["The general notes in the bottom corner of the sheet","The revision entry and date in the title block","The legend with all of its telecom symbols","The scale printed under the floor plan drawing"],c:1,e:"The title block carries the sheet number and the revision. Always confirm the latest revision before you build from a drawing."},
{id:"u1l5-2",t:"u1l5",q:"A symbol of a triangle with a filled corner appears on the sheet. What decodes it?",a:["The foreman's memory of the last job on the same floor","The standard symbol chart, because a triangle always means a voice outlet","The legend on that sheet, since symbols differ from job to job","The reflected ceiling plan, which repeats every symbol in the building"],c:2,e:"Each sheet's legend decodes its own symbols. The same shape can mean different things on another job, so read the legend first."},
{id:"u1l5-3",t:"u1l5",q:"The plan shows an outlet on a wall that is not there in the field because a duct occupies the space. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Move the outlet to the nearest clear wall and update the label to match","Install the outlet on the duct so that it is at the right spot","Wait for the inspector, who will tell you where the outlet should be","Stop, ask the foreman to get the designer's answer in writing, and mark the change on the as-built set"],c:3,e:"When the drawing and the field disagree, stop and ask the designer, then document the change so the record matches what was built."},
{id:"u1l5-4",t:"u1l5",q:"Which sheet shows how the floors connect and the backbone between telecom rooms?",a:["The riser diagram, which shows the connection between floors","A schedule, which lists the outlets and the cable in a table","A floor plan, which shows outlets and rooms on a single level","A reflected ceiling plan, which shows devices and lights in the ceiling"],c:0,e:"A riser diagram shows how floors connect and the backbone between TRs. A floor plan shows one level, and a schedule is a table."},
{id:"u1l5-5",t:"u1l5",q:"You need a table listing every outlet ID and the cable to it. Where do you look?",a:["A detail sheet showing the rack mounting at a larger scale of the drawing","A schedule","The title block, which carries the outlet counts for the whole floor","The reflected ceiling plan, which lists every device that hangs in a ceiling"],c:1,e:"Schedules are tables that list outlets, cable, or equipment. Check that the schedule and floor plan agree."},

// u1l6
{id:"u1l6-1",t:"u1l6",q:"A delivery arrives with one crushed carton and the driver wants a signature. What do you do before signing?",a:["Sign and open the carton later when the foreman has time to look","Refuse the entire pallet and send the driver away with it","Note the damage on the slip","Sign without a note, since the crushed box is only a packaging issue"],c:2,e:"Check the delivery and write the damage on the delivery slip before signing. Once signed clean, the shortage or damage is hard to claim."},
{id:"u1l6-2",t:"u1l6",q:"The box label says CMP, but the jacket on the first reel reads CMR, and the ceiling is an air return. What do you do?",a:["Install it, since riser cable is close enough to plenum cable for a ceiling","Install it above the grid only, where the inspector will not see the print","Cut it into short sections so the jacket print cannot be read at all","Reject the reel, because riser cable cannot replace plenum cable in an air-handling ceiling"],c:3,e:"The jacket print decides, not the carton. A lower rating can never replace a higher one, so a CMR reel cannot go in a plenum."},
{id:"u1l6-3",t:"u1l6",q:"Which tool seats and trims a wire on an insulation displacement connector?",a:["A punch-down tool, with the blade matched to the type of block","A crimper, with dies matched to the modular plug that is used, which many installers prefer to do","A fish tape, which pulls the wire down between the contacts, and then move on to the next task","A label maker, which prints the identifier for the contact"],c:0,e:"A punch-down tool seats the wire into the IDC and trims it. A crimper attaches plugs and a fish tape pulls string or cable."},
{id:"u1l6-4",t:"u1l6",q:"The footage marks on a reel read 3100 at the start of a pull and 2650 at the end. How much cable was pulled?",a:["5750 ft, since the two marks are added together on a pull","450 ft, since the marks count down from 3100 to 2650","3100 ft, since the first mark is the footage on the reel","2650 ft, since the last mark is always the length pulled"],c:1,e:"Footage marks on this reel count down, so the pull used 3100 - 2650 = 450 ft. Recording marks at each end of every pull makes waste and shortages visible."},
{id:"u1l6-5",t:"u1l6",q:"Where should reels be staged overnight?",a:["Outside the building by the dock where the next delivery crew will find them","In the corridor so that every trade can see how much cable is left","Covered and off a wet floor","In the ceiling above the work area where they are out of the way"],c:2,e:"Keep reels covered, off a floor that can get wet, and where the crew can find them and strangers cannot."},

// u1l7
{id:"u1l7-1",t:"u1l7",q:"A tech finds a cable labeled only at the TR end, with the outlet end bare. What is wrong with that labeling?",a:["Nothing, because the panel record is enough to identify the outlet end, a shortcut that crews often take","Only the outlet end needs a label since the TR already has a panel","The TR end label should have been handwritten so it can be corrected, as is the usual habit on many sites","Both ends need the same identifier, and it names both ends of the cable"],c:3,e:"Every cable is labeled at both ends with the same identifier, and that identifier names both ends so either end leads to the other."},
{id:"u1l7-2",t:"u1l7",q:"Which label placement and material is the BEST practice?",a:["A machine-printed, durable label placed within sight of the end where it can be read without moving the cable","Printed on a sheet taped to the wall behind each panel in the room","Handwritten in marker directly on the jacket near the last bundle tie","Handwritten on tape, hung from the cable in a loose flag at the end"],c:0,e:"Machine-printed, durable labels placed within sight of the end can be read without moving the cable. Handwriting smudges and flags fall off."},
{id:"u1l7-3",t:"u1l7",q:"A cable identifier on this job reads 3A-PP2-14 / 3-306-B. What does it tell a tech?",a:["Room 14 on floor 3 and room 306 on panel B","Panel port 14 on panel 2 in TR 3A, and outlet B in room 306 on floor 3","Fourteen cables from TR 3A and two cables to room 306, which many installers prefer to do","TR 3A holds fourteen panels and room 306 holds two outlets labeled B, as is the usual habit on many sites"],c:1,e:"A cable identifier names both ends. Here the panel end is port 14 on panel 2 in TR 3A, and the outlet end is port B in room 306 on floor 3."},
{id:"u1l7-4",t:"u1l7",q:"Which items must agree with the label before handoff?",a:["The tester file alone, because the label may be replaced later","The label and the invoice only, since the other records are optional","The label, the drawing, the panel record, and the tester file, each repeating the same identifier for the cable","The label and the customer's phone list, which is used for moves"],c:2,e:"The label, drawing, panel record, and tester file must all agree. A mismatch makes a defect that wastes a tech's time later."},
{id:"u1l7-5",t:"u1l7",q:"Which items on the job need a unique identifier in the labeling scheme?",a:["Only the outlets and the cables, since the rest are visible by their location","Every cable and the faceplates, with the TR identified by a sign","Only the items the inspector asks about at the final walk-through","Every space, panel, port, cable, pathway, and bonding conductor"],c:3,e:"A scheme gives a unique identifier to every space, panel, port, cable, pathway, and bonding conductor, all in one consistent pattern."},

// u2l1
{id:"u2l1-1",t:"u2l1",q:"The inspector says a cable must come out, though you point out that the standard allows it. Who wins?",a:["The code, enforced by the AHJ","The standard, because it was written by technical experts in the field","The foreman, who has the last word on any dispute with an inspector","The manufacturer, whose installation sheet overrides any local rule"],c:0,e:"A code is law once adopted, and the AHJ enforces it. A standard binds only when a code or contract calls for it."},
{id:"u2l1-2",t:"u2l1",q:"A subcontract says to install cabling per a TIA standard. What does that do to the standard?",a:["Nothing, since a standard is only advice and never has any force on a job","It makes the standard binding on the job, because the contract calls for it","It turns the standard into a law that applies to every building in the state","It makes the standard apply only to the cable, not to pathways or spaces"],c:1,e:"A standard is a consensus document that becomes enforceable when a code, contract, or specification calls for it."},
{id:"u2l1-3",t:"u2l1",q:"A product carries an NRTL label such as UL. What does that tell the inspector?",a:["It is guaranteed to meet the manufacturer's warranty in every building","It is approved for any use as long as the installer follows the print","It is listed for a stated use and may be installed only for that use","It has been banned from use in plenums until the lab retests it"],c:2,e:"An NRTL tests a product and lists it for a stated use. A listing for one use does not make the product acceptable for another."},
{id:"u2l1-4",t:"u2l1",q:"The drawing calls for conduit and pathway work. Which standard family covers pathways and spaces?",a:["TIA-568, which covers topology, distances, performance, and test methods","TIA-607, which covers bonding and grounding for telecommunications","TIA-606, which covers administration, labels, and the records you keep","TIA-569"],c:3,e:"TIA-569 covers pathways and spaces. TIA-568 covers the cabling, TIA-606 administration, and TIA-607 bonding."},
{id:"u2l1-5",t:"u2l1",q:"The drawing, a standard, and the local code each give a different separation distance. Which controls?",a:["The stricter requirement, and the AHJ settles anything unclear after you ask in writing","The drawing, since the designer wrote it and the inspector reads it","The shortest distance, because it saves the owner money on the cable","The standard, because it is newer than the drawing and the local code"],c:0,e:"The stricter requirement wins, and local codes can be stricter than national ones. Ask the designer in writing and let the AHJ settle anything unclear."},

// u2l2
{id:"u2l2-1",t:"u2l2",q:"Which cable rating may be installed in a plenum ceiling?",a:["CMR, riser rated, which suits vertical runs between floors","CMP, plenum rated, the highest rating","CMX, limited use, which suits short runs in dwellings and raceways","CM, general purpose, which suits horizontal runs on a single floor"],c:1,e:"Plenum spaces require plenum (CMP) cable, the highest rating. Riser, general, and limited-use cable have lower ratings."},
{id:"u2l2-2",t:"u2l2",q:"Leftover CMG is on the cart, and the next run goes through a plenum. What do you do?",a:["Install it, since it is only a short run above one room","Install it inside a short piece of conduit to hide the print","Do not install it","Tape a CMP label on the jacket and tell the foreman it is plenum"],c:2,e:"General cable can never replace plenum cable, even for a short run. A missing or false rating is a violation the inspector will find."},
{id:"u2l2-3",t:"u2l2",q:"A reel of CMR remains and the next run is on one floor with no air handling above. May you use it?",a:["No, since only general rated cable may be used on a single floor","No, because riser cable is made only for vertical shafts and risers","Yes, but only after you cover the print with a general-cable label","Yes, since a higher rating may replace a lower one"],c:3,e:"A higher rating may replace a lower one, so riser cable may go where general cable is required. The reverse is never allowed."},
{id:"u2l2-4",t:"u2l2",q:"The jacket print on a cable is missing and the reel has no box. What is the BEST action?",a:["Treat it as unrated and do not install it","Use it in the ceiling, since the manufacturer's color suggests plenum","Test it with a cable tester, and install it if it passes the test","Use it only in a short run where the inspector will not look"],c:0,e:"Without a print there is no proof of the rating. Treat the cable as unrated, because the rating comes from the listing printed on the jacket."},
{id:"u2l2-5",t:"u2l2",q:"Which describes CMX cable?",a:["Plenum rated cable for the air-handling spaces of every commercial building","Limited use cable, for short runs in dwellings and raceways, and not for plenums, risers, or general commercial runs","General purpose cable for horizontal runs in every commercial building","Riser rated cable for vertical runs between floors in a firestopped shaft"],c:1,e:"CMX is the lowest rating. It is limited to dwellings and raceways in short runs, and cannot replace CMG, CMR, or CMP."},

// u3l1
{id:"u3l1-1",t:"u3l1",q:"What is the maximum length of a horizontal permanent link?",a:["100 m (328 ft), because the permanent link includes both cords at the ends","10 m (33 ft), because the cords make up the link between outlet and TR","90 m (295 ft)","30 m (100 ft), which is the limit between pull points in conduit"],c:2,e:"The permanent link is 90 m (295 ft). Add up to 10 m of cords and the channel is 100 m (328 ft)."},
{id:"u3l1-2",t:"u3l1",q:"Two TRs on the same floor are connected by a cable. How is that cabling classified?",a:["Horizontal cabling, because both rooms are on the same floor of the building","Work area cabling, because it ends at equipment in each room of the floor","An interconnect, because it joins two panels without a patch cord between","Backbone"],c:3,e:"Cabling between two TRs is backbone, even when they are on one floor. Horizontal cabling runs from the outlet to the TR."},
{id:"u3l1-3",t:"u3l1",q:"How many levels of cross-connect are allowed in horizontal cabling?",a:["One","Two, one at the TR and one at the work area in a consolidation panel","Three, one at each floor and one in the equipment room","Any number, as long as every panel is labeled at the front"],c:0,e:"Horizontal cabling allows one cross-connect level only, with no tandem patch panels between the outlet and the TR."},
{id:"u3l1-4",t:"u3l1",q:"What is not allowed in horizontal copper cabling?",a:["A patch panel in the TR with a label at the front of each port","A bridged tap or a splice","A cord of a few meters at the work area outlet to the equipment","A cable with a jacket print that shows the category and rating"],c:1,e:"Bridged taps and splices are not allowed in horizontal copper cabling. The permanent link runs unbroken from the outlet to the panel."},
{id:"u3l1-5",t:"u3l1",q:"A 78 m permanent link has 8 m of cords in total. What is the channel length, and does it pass?",a:["70 m, which is within the limit of the cords alone","100 m, which is the full channel limit with the cords added","86 m, which is under the 100 m channel limit","98 m, which is over the permanent link limit of 90 m"],c:2,e:"Channel length = link + cords = 78 + 8 = 86 m. That is under 100 m, and the link of 78 m is under 90 m, so it passes."},

// u3l2
{id:"u3l2-1",t:"u3l2",q:"The farthest outlet on a floor will be 96 m of cable from the planned TR. What is the BEST response?",a:["Pull it anyway, since the cords add only 10 m, which is a small amount","Pull a thinner cable so that the signal loss stays within the limit","Ask the user to plug in a long cord that is 10 m longer than normal","Move the TR or add one so that no horizontal run passes 90 m"],c:3,e:"No horizontal run may pass 90 m, so the room has to move or another TR or TE is needed. Cords cannot make up the difference."},
{id:"u3l2-2",t:"u3l2",q:"A TE is planned for a zone on one floor. Which statement about it is correct?",a:["It serves at most 335 square meters (3600 square feet) on a floor and never replaces a TR","It serves a zone of any size, so long as it is locked","It serves any floor if the TR is full of equipment","It replaces the TR for any floor smaller than the first floor"],c:0,e:"A TE serves at most 335 square meters (3600 square feet) on a floor. It is a cabinet for a small zone and never a substitute for a TR."},
{id:"u3l2-3",t:"u3l2",q:"Which of these does not belong in a TR?",a:["A lockable door wide enough to move equipment in or out","A water pipe over the racks","Conditioned air and clean, dry floors in the room","Dedicated, nonswitched power outlets for the equipment"],c:1,e:"Water pipes or drains over equipment, unrelated equipment, and storage do not belong in a TR. The room needs a lock, clean air, power, and bonding."},
{id:"u3l2-4",t:"u3l2",q:"You are mounting the plywood backboard in a TR. Which choice matches the rule?",a:["Void-free 12 mm plywood on the ceiling, left unpainted so the stamp shows","Any thin plywood on one wall, painted black to hide the grain of the wood","Void-free 19 mm (3/4 in) plywood on at least two walls, with two coats of fire-retardant paint unless the code requires treated plywood","Sheets of particleboard on every wall, painted white to reflect the light"],c:2,e:"The backboard is void-free plywood, usually 19 mm (3/4 in), on at least two walls, with two coats of fire-retardant paint. Treated plywood is not painted."},
{id:"u3l2-5",t:"u3l2",q:"The architect asks where the TRs belong in a three-story building. What is the rule?",a:["One for every ten outlets, wherever the floor space is available","One in the basement that serves all three floors through long cables","One on the middle floor only, with the other floors served by cords","At least one per floor, placed so that no horizontal run passes 90 m"],c:3,e:"There is at least one TR per floor, on the same floor as the work areas it serves, and it is placed so that no horizontal run passes 90 m."},

// u3l3
{id:"u3l3-1",t:"u3l3",q:"A new floor-standing rack arrives. Which is the correct way to secure it?",a:["Bolt it to the floor with expansion anchors, flat washers, and lock washers, and brace the top","Set it on the floor and rely on the weight of the equipment to hold it","Screw it to the wall with drywall screws and leave the floor free","Tie it to the nearest cable tray with cable ties after the cables are in"],c:0,e:"Anchor a floor rack with expansion anchors, flat washers, and lock washers, and brace the top to the wall, a tray, or a ladder rack."},
{id:"u3l3-2",t:"u3l3",q:"A crew wants to push a rack tight against the back wall to save floor space. What is the problem?",a:["Racks must stand 30 cm from the wall so that the air can circulate","The front and rear must remain accessible so the equipment can be serviced","Racks cannot touch the wall because the bonding conductor would be short, as most crews do on a busy day","Nothing, since all racks are serviced from the front side in every room, on the theory that it is good enough"],c:1,e:"Use double-sided racks and keep both the front and the rear accessible. A rack tight against a wall cannot be serviced."},
{id:"u3l3-3",t:"u3l3",q:"A crew hangs a bundle of two hundred cables from D-rings. What is wrong with that choice?",a:["D-rings are banned in telecom rooms by code in every jurisdiction, since that is quicker for the whole crew","D-rings are used only in the entrance facility and never in a TR","D-rings suit small bundles and jumpers, and a large bundle needs a ladder rack","Nothing, since D-rings may carry any number of cables if they are labeled, since that is quicker for the whole crew"],c:2,e:"D-rings are fine for small bundles and cross-connect jumpers, but they never replace a ladder rack for a large bundle of cable."},
{id:"u3l3-4",t:"u3l3",q:"How much vertical space is one rack unit (1 U)?",a:["About 25 mm (1 in)","About 90 mm (3.5 in)","About 150 mm (6 in)","About 45 mm (1.75 in)"],c:3,e:"One rack unit is about 45 mm (1.75 in). A piece of equipment is described by the number of rack units it occupies."},
{id:"u3l3-5",t:"u3l3",q:"A small zone gets a wall-mounted rack. What is the BEST way to mount it?",a:["Lag bolts and washers into the plywood, after checking that the wall will carry the full load","Adhesive strips rated for light equipment, with the top braced to a pipe","Screws into the drywall alone, spaced about every foot along the sides","Plastic anchors into the plaster, with the heaviest equipment at the top"],c:0,e:"A wall rack goes on lag bolts and washers into the plywood, and the wall has to carry the full load of equipment and cable."},

// u3l4
{id:"u3l4-1",t:"u3l4",q:"Which conductor joins the primary bonding busbar to the building's grounding electrode?",a:["The TBB, which links the PBB to the busbar in each telecom room","The BCT","The grounding equalizer, which evens potentials between TRs","The rack bonding conductor, which runs from the busbar to each rack"],c:1,e:"The bonding conductor for telecommunications (BCT) joins the PBB to the grounding electrode and is kept as short as possible."},
{id:"u3l4-2",t:"u3l4",q:"An old drawing shows a TMGB in the entrance facility. What is the current name for it?",a:["The secondary bonding busbar, which is the busbar found in each floor's TR","The rack bonding conductor, which replaced the old name for it on each rack","The primary bonding busbar (PBB), the one busbar for the building in the entrance facility","The telecommunications bonding backbone, which runs up the riser"],c:2,e:"The TMGB is now the primary bonding busbar (PBB). The TGB is now the secondary bonding busbar (SBB)."},
{id:"u3l4-3",t:"u3l4",q:"Which conductor links the PBB to each SBB on the floors?",a:["The BCT","The TMGB","The TEBC","The TBB"],c:3,e:"The telecommunications bonding backbone (TBB) links the PBB to each SBB and keeps every floor at the same potential."},
{id:"u3l4-4",t:"u3l4",q:"An old copper water pipe runs near the TR. May it be the telecom ground path?",a:["Do not use it as the telecom ground path on its own, because the path can be broken","Yes, because metal pipes are the best path to the earth in every building, since that is quicker for the whole crew","Yes, if the plumber signs off on the pipe after a test of the joints, as is the usual habit on many sites","Only for the racks that sit closest to the pipe in the TR"],c:0,e:"A water pipe is not a dependable bonding path on its own, since the pipe can be broken or replaced with plastic. Use the telecom bonding system."},
{id:"u3l4-5",t:"u3l4",q:"Which items are bonded to the telecom bonding system?",a:["Only the racks, since all the other metal parts are bonded by the electrician","Racks, cable tray, shields, armored cable, and the entrance protector","The ceiling grid and light fixtures along each pathway in the corridor","Plastic conduit and cable ties used to support the cables in the ceiling"],c:1,e:"Racks, cable tray and ladder rack, shields, armored cable, and the entrance protector are all bonded, each with a conductor back to the busbar."},

// u3l5
{id:"u3l5-1",t:"u3l5",q:"A rack's mounting surface is painted. How do you make the bonding connection?",a:["Bolt the lug over the paint and tighten it firmly with a wrench","Wrap the conductor around the rack leg and tape it in place","Scrape the paint to bare metal, apply antioxidant compound, then make the connection with a two-hole compression lug","Use a lug that is made for a larger conductor so it grips the paint"],c:2,e:"Paint insulates. Scrape to bare metal, apply antioxidant compound, and bolt a two-hole compression lug to make a joint that lasts."},
{id:"u3l5-2",t:"u3l5",q:"Three racks stand in a row. The crew wants to run one conductor to the first rack and jump to the next two. Is that allowed?",a:["Yes, because a single conductor is shorter and is easier to label","Yes, if the jumpers are made of a larger conductor than the main","No, but only the last rack in a row needs its own conductor","No; each rack gets its own conductor back to the busbar"],c:3,e:"Daisy-chaining is never done, since one loose joint takes every later rack off the bond. Each rack gets its own conductor back to the busbar."},
{id:"u3l5-3",t:"u3l5",q:"How should a bonding conductor be routed from the busbar to a rack?",a:["The shortest path, with no bend tighter than 90 degrees and a radius of at least 203 mm (8 in)","Along the longest path, to keep it clear of the cable pathways","Coiled into loops behind the rack so extra length is always on hand","Through a tight bend at each corner, held down with cable staples"],c:0,e:"Route it by the shortest path with no sharp bends. The minimum bend radius is 203 mm (8 in) and it is never bent to less than 90 degrees."},
{id:"u3l5-4",t:"u3l5",q:"Who verifies bonding resistance and records it after the bonding is complete?",a:["The cable installer, using a continuity beeper on the cable pairs","The electrician or the testing technician, against the recommended maximum of 0.1 ohm","The inspector, who measures it after the ceiling tiles are replaced, and then move on to the next task","Nobody, since a visual check is all the standards ask for on a job, which saves time on the job"],c:1,e:"You make the joint and check it visually. The electrician or testing technician measures the resistance, with 0.1 ohm (100 milliohms) as the recommended maximum."},
{id:"u3l5-5",t:"u3l5",q:"What jacket color marks a bonding conductor?",a:["Red, so the conductor stands out from the cables in the tray","White, so that it matches the telecom room walls and the labels","Green","Blue, so the conductor matches the color of the data cables"],c:2,e:"Every bonding conductor is green, or green with a yellow stripe, so nobody mistakes it for a signal or power conductor."},

// u3l6
{id:"u3l6-1",t:"u3l6",q:"Two hundred cables must reach a TR through a ceiling space. Which pathway fits BEST?",a:["A row of J-hooks, since they cost less than any other support type","Surface raceway, since it can be mounted on the corridor wall","A single conduit, since conduit gives the best protection to every cable","A cable tray, since it carries many cables with open access from above"],c:3,e:"A cable tray carries many cables with open access from above. J-hooks suit small bundles, and conduit is meant for protection and small counts."},
{id:"u3l6-2",t:"u3l6",q:"A crew hung J-hooks from the ceiling grid wires because they were close. What should be done?",a:["Remove them and hang independent supports from the structure above, because grid wires belong to another trade","Leave them, since the grid wires carry the weight of the bundle without problems","Add a second hook next to each one so that the weight is spread out","Tie the cable bundle to the grid with ties so that the hooks carry less load"],c:0,e:"Never attach to ceiling grid wires, the grid, sprinkler pipes, or another trade's supports. Hang from the structure above with your own rod, strap, or beam clamp."},
{id:"u3l6-3",t:"u3l6",q:"A new open pathway runs 600 mm from unshielded power on a circuit above 5 kVA. Is that acceptable?",a:["Yes, because 600 mm is more than the 305 mm required for every pathway, a shortcut that crews often take","It is too close; the minimum is 610 mm, so move the pathway or change the pathway type","No, because the rule is 1220 mm for every open pathway near power","Yes, because the cable is shielded and shielded cable ignores the distance, which saves time on the job"],c:1,e:"For unshielded power in an open or nonmetal pathway the minimum is 610 mm (24 in), so 600 mm falls short. A grounded metal pathway reduces the distance."},
{id:"u3l6-4",t:"u3l6",q:"What is the minimum separation from electric motors and transformers?",a:["305 mm (12 in), which is the figure for power beside metal","130 mm (5.12 in), which is the figure for lighting","1220 mm (48 in) from electric motors and transformers","610 mm (24 in), which is the figure for open pathways"],c:2,e:"Electric motors and transformers need 1220 mm (48 in). The other distances belong to fluorescent lighting and to power cables in various pathways."},
{id:"u3l6-5",t:"u3l6",q:"Which of these is a common source of EMI near a pathway?",a:["A plastic duct","A ceiling tile","A sprinkler pipe","A large motor"],c:3,e:"Motors, transformers, fluorescent fixtures, and power cables create electromagnetic interference. Keep cable away from them."},

// u3l7
{id:"u3l7-1",t:"u3l7",q:"Two Category 6A cables go to a double-gang faceplate, and the crew has shallow boxes on the cart. What is the BEST choice?",a:["A deep double-gang box, so the cable keeps its bend radius","A single-gang box with one cable and a blank for the other jack","A shallow box with the cables folded in tightly to fit the space","A low-voltage bracket in the open wall with no box at all"],c:0,e:"Category 6A is stiff and large. A deep box leaves room for the bend radius, and a shallow box forces a tight bend that fails the test later."},
{id:"u3l7-2",t:"u3l7",q:"Who decides the mounting height of the outlets on a project?",a:["The installer, who sets all the heights to the same standard height","The drawings and the AHJ, including accessibility rules","The electrician, who picks the height of every outlet at the same time","The cable manufacturer, whose sheet gives one height for all rooms"],c:1,e:"Heights come from the drawings and the AHJ, including accessibility rules. Never guess the height, and measure it from the finished floor."},
{id:"u3l7-3",t:"u3l7",q:"A low-voltage mounting bracket is planned for a wall that is a fire barrier. What do you do?",a:["Install it, since it is only a small opening in the wall, as most crews do on a busy day","Install it and seal the gap behind it with ordinary caulk, since that is quicker for the whole crew","Use it only if it is listed for that fire or smoke barrier","Cut a larger hole so that a metal box can sit behind it"],c:2,e:"A low-voltage bracket is never used in a fire or smoke barrier unless it is listed for it, and its firestop is installed as the listing says."},
{id:"u3l7-4",t:"u3l7",q:"Drywall crews come tomorrow and the boxes are set. What should you do at rough-in?",a:["Fill each box with insulation so the drywall will not touch the cable","Leave the boxes open and clean them with a vacuum after the walls are done","Remove the boxes and set them again after the walls have been finished","Cover or tape each box opening so drywall mud stays out, and leave a pull string in any conduit stub-up"],c:3,e:"Cover the boxes so mud and dust stay out, and leave a pull string in each stub-up so the cable can be pulled later."},
{id:"u3l7-5",t:"u3l7",q:"A faceplate has an unused position when the outlet is finished. What do you do?",a:["Fill it with a blank and label the outlet","Cover it with tape, which you can remove when a jack is installed","Cut the faceplate to a smaller size to remove the open position","Leave it open so a jack can be added later without a tool"],c:0,e:"Fill every unused position with a blank so dust stays out, and label the outlet."},

// u3l8
{id:"u3l8-1",t:"u3l8",q:"A customer wants a new outlet in a finished wall. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Cut the opening","Locate studs and check the cavity","Level the ring","Drop a string"],c:1,e:"Before any cut, locate the studs and check the cavity for power and obstructions. A saw that meets a wire or a stud ruins the wall and may injure you."},
{id:"u3l8-2",t:"u3l8",q:"The wall for a cut-in ring is marked on the drawings as fire rated. What do you do?",a:["Use a standard plastic ring and caulk the edges after the cable is in, which many installers prefer to do","Skip the ring and run the cable through the wall without a box","Use only a box that is listed for the rated wall and firestop it to the listing","Use a metal ring from the truck, since metal is always acceptable, since that is quicker for the whole crew"],c:2,e:"A fire-rated wall needs a box listed for it and a firestop installed to its listing. A common plastic ring does not keep the wall's rating."},
{id:"u3l8-3",t:"u3l8",q:"The opening is cut and you need the cable at the wall. What is the BEST way to get it there?",a:["Push the cable down the wall from the ceiling and hope it lands","Tape the cable to a broom handle and push it into the wall cavity","Cut a second opening near the floor and fish the cable up the cavity","Drop a string or fish tape from the ceiling space and pull the cable to the opening"],c:3,e:"Drop a string or run a fish tape between the ceiling space and the opening, tie on the cable, and pull it through."},
{id:"u3l8-4",t:"u3l8",q:"The ring is set in the customer's office and the crew packs up. What cleanup do you owe?",a:["Vacuum the dust as you go, bag the scraps, and leave the customer's wall and furniture clean","Wipe the faceplate only, since the ceiling debris is not seen by the user","Leave the scraps in a box for the janitor to take away in the morning","Ask the customer to clean up and send a bill for any extra time"],c:0,e:"Cut-in work makes drywall dust. Vacuum as you work, bag the scraps, and leave the space clean, since you are working in an occupied room."},
{id:"u3l8-5",t:"u3l8",q:"After the ring is placed in the opening it wobbles. What is the correct way to secure it?",a:["Pack the gap with paper and tape the ring flush with the wall","Tighten the wings until the ring grips the drywall","Glue the ring to the wall and remove the wings afterward","Screw the ring into the drywall at each corner of the faceplate"],c:1,e:"A cut-in ring clamps to the drywall with its wings. Tighten them until the ring grips firmly, and keep the opening no larger than needed."},

// u3l9
{id:"u3l9-1",t:"u3l9",q:"A conference table in the middle of a rated floor needs data under it. Which statement about a poke-through is correct?",a:["It is an ordinary box that never affects the rating of the floor","It keeps the rating whenever the core hole is filled with foam afterward","It keeps the floor's rating only when installed exactly to its listing","It needs no listing as long as the hole is smaller than the cable bundle"],c:2,e:"A poke-through is a listed, fire-rated device. It restores the floor's rating only when installed exactly as its listing says."},
{id:"u3l9-2",t:"u3l9",q:"A poke-through feeds the plenum ceiling of the floor below. What cable must go through it?",a:["Any general purpose cable, since it only crosses the floor for a short run","Riser cable, since the cable passes from one floor to the next","Limited use cable, since the run is short and in a protected device","Plenum rated cable, because the cable enters the plenum below"],c:3,e:"The cable must match the space it enters. A plenum below means plenum cable through the poke-through."},
{id:"u3l9-3",t:"u3l9",q:"The designer shows a floor core for a new poke-through. Where may you core?",a:["Only where the drawings show it, after the slab has been scanned","Wherever the floor looks clear of obstacles under the carpet","Anywhere near the table, since the exact spot is the installer's call","Next to a column, since the slab is thicker there and stronger"],c:0,e:"Core only where the drawings show, and scan the slab first. Never cut into structure without approval."},
{id:"u3l9-4",t:"u3l9",q:"Cables run under an access floor to the new outlets. What is the BEST method?",a:["Lay them loose on the slab, in loose piles of cable between the pedestals, since that is quicker for the whole crew","Support them in trays or baskets, separated from power, keeping the airflow clear","Bundle them tightly against the pedestals with ties for support","Run them in the same tray as the power so one pathway does the job, which saves time on the job"],c:1,e:"Under an access floor use trays or baskets, keep the separation from power, and keep airflow clear, since piles of cable block cooling."},
{id:"u3l9-5",t:"u3l9",q:"A floor box sits in a rated slab. What is required after the cabling is in?",a:["Nothing, since the box cover keeps dust and water out of the box, as most crews do on a busy day","A bead of ordinary caulk around the box, which is inspected later, since that is quicker for the whole crew","It is firestopped and sealed to the listing, with a cover that keeps out water and dust","The box is removed and the core is filled with a sand mix"],c:2,e:"A floor box in a rated slab is firestopped and sealed to its listing, and its cover keeps out water and dust."},

// u3l10
{id:"u3l10-1",t:"u3l10",q:"Cubicles are fed from a power pole at the ceiling. How do you arrange power and communications?",a:["Put the communications on the same side as power to save the trouble","Run both in the same channel so the cables can be dressed together","Run power on the outside and put the data cable under the power","Keep power and communications on separate sides of the pole or raceway to reduce interference"],c:3,e:"Power and communications stay on separate sides of a pole or furniture raceway, which limits interference and keeps the two systems apart."},
{id:"u3l10-2",t:"u3l10",q:"Category 6A cables fail NEXT after being routed through tight furniture raceways. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["A bend tighter than the radius, which can be avoided by using the wide channel","The cables are too short to reach the outlet in the cubicle, and then move on to the next task","The jacks were made for stranded cable, which loses signal","The jacks were punched with the wrong tool in the work area, and then move on to the next task"],c:0,e:"Furniture raceways are tight. A bend sharper than the cable's bend radius, especially for Category 6A, causes test failures, so use the wide channel and leave no kinks."},
{id:"u3l10-3",t:"u3l10",q:"A wireless access point is planned above a plenum ceiling tile. What is the BEST method for the cable?",a:["General purpose cable laid on the ceiling tile with a small service loop","Plenum rated cable to a plenum rated enclosure, hung from the structure","Riser cable hung from the grid wire and tied with a plastic cable tie","Any cable, since the access point itself carries a plenum rating"],c:1,e:"The ceiling is a plenum, so use plenum cable and a plenum rated enclosure, supported from the structure and not from the grid."},
{id:"u3l10-4",t:"u3l10",q:"An outlet is planned on an exterior wall. Which box is required?",a:["A single-gang metal box with a standard plastic faceplate","A deep box with a blank, held on with a plain adhesive","A weatherproof box","A low-voltage bracket in the open stud space of the wall"],c:2,e:"Wet and exterior locations need a weatherproof box with a gasketed cover and sealed entries, and a drip loop in the cable."},
{id:"u3l10-5",t:"u3l10",q:"A cable enters a weatherproof box from above in the rain. How do you keep water out of the box?",a:["Seal the cover with tape and leave the cable straight to the box","Run the cable in a straight line and caulk the entry only on the inside","Point the cable upward so that the water runs back toward the roof","Form a drip loop so water drips off the cable before it reaches the box"],c:3,e:"Water follows the cable, so form a drip loop. The cable hangs low before it rises into the box, and the water drips off at the bottom."},

// u3l11
{id:"u3l11-1",t:"u3l11",q:"The drawing calls for a new core through a slab. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Scan the slab for rebar and conduit and clear the location","Mark the hole with paint and begin cutting from the top floor","Start the drill with water running to keep the dust down","Ask the customer which side of the room they prefer for the core"],c:0,e:"Scan the slab for rebar, conduit, and post-tension cables before coring. Never cut into structure without the designer's approval."},
{id:"u3l11-2",t:"u3l11",q:"A sleeve is planned for a pull of cables that will grow later. How should it be filled today?",a:["Packed full, since the sleeve is firestopped and cannot be changed later, as most crews do on a busy day","With spare capacity left for growth, not filled to its limit on the first day","Filled to the limit and then fitted with a second sleeve on the next floor, which saves time on the job","Left empty, since cable cannot be placed until the firestop is installed"],c:1,e:"Size sleeves for growth. A sleeve filled to its limit leaves the next pull with no room, and the firestop makes changes harder."},
{id:"u3l11-3",t:"u3l11",q:"A sleeve end is raw metal where a cable will pass. What do you add?",a:["Tape around the edge","A rag in the hole","A bushing","A coat of paint"],c:2,e:"A bushing at the sleeve ends protects the jacket from the cut edge."},
{id:"u3l11-4",t:"u3l11",q:"An unused sleeve passes through a fire-rated wall. What should be done with it?",a:["Leave it open so air can move between the rooms in the building","Seal it only on one side, since the other side is not visible","Fill it with foam and leave the wall without a label for the inspector","Cap and label it, and firestop it too if the wall is rated"],c:3,e:"Cap and label unused openings, and firestop them too when the wall or floor is rated. An open opening breaks the rating."},
{id:"u3l11-5",t:"u3l11",q:"A riser slot has just been framed and the backbone cables are installed. What is the next requirement?",a:["Firestop it with a listed system after the cabling is in place","Leave it open until the inspector asks to see the finished framing","Fill it with insulation so the cables stay in place in the slot","Cover it with a metal plate and leave the cables as they are in the slot"],c:0,e:"Every slot with cable through it gets a listed firestop system after cabling, framed as the listing requires."},

// u3l12
{id:"u3l12-1",t:"u3l12",q:"A new ladder rack carries only low-voltage cable. What do you do about bonding?",a:["Skip it","Bond every section","Bond only the first section","Bond it at one end only"],c:1,e:"Every tray and ladder rack section is bonded, whether it carries power or low-voltage cable."},
{id:"u3l12-2",t:"u3l12",q:"A listed tray is a few inches too long for the corridor. What should the crew do?",a:["Cut it with a hacksaw and file the edge, since it is only one section","Cut it and leave a note on the drawing for the next trade","Ask the AHJ first, because a field cut can void the listing","Splice a shorter piece from scrap tray on the other end"],c:2,e:"A field cut can void the tray's listing, so ask the AHJ before cutting a listed tray."},
{id:"u3l12-3",t:"u3l12",q:"A tray is planned with room for growth. Which initial fill is a common design target?",a:["Fill it to 90 percent so the cable bundle holds together","Fill it completely, since cable can be removed later if needed","Fill it to 50 percent, since half of the tray is open for later cables","25 percent"],c:3,e:"A common planning target is to start no more than 25 percent full. At 50 percent the cables completely fill the tray."},
{id:"u3l12-4",t:"u3l12",q:"A vertical ladder rack carries heavy backbone cable up through a riser. How do you hold the cable?",a:["Secure each cable to the rung","Tie every cable at the top only, where the pull ends","Rely on the friction of the cables against one another in the bundle","Hang each cable by its own jacket from the rack's top rung"],c:0,e:"Secure each cable to the rung so the weight spreads over several supports instead of hanging on one point."},
{id:"u3l12-5",t:"u3l12",q:"A tray 12 in wide by 3.94 in deep holds 120 cables of 0.30 in OD. What is the fill, and is it within a 25 percent planning target?",a:["About 35 percent, which is over the target and needs a larger tray","About 17.9 percent, which is under the 25 percent planning target","About 8 percent, which is well under the target and needs no care","About 50 percent, which is the point at which the tray is completely full"],c:1,e:"Cable area is 120 x 0.0707 = 8.48 square in over a tray area of 47.28 square in, which is 17.9 percent, under the 25 percent planning target."},

// u3l13
{id:"u3l13-1",t:"u3l13",q:"A 24 m corridor run uses J-hooks. How should they be placed?",a:["Eleven hooks spaced about every 2.2 m so the sag stays shallow","Seventeen hooks spaced evenly at exactly 1.5 m with no variation","At least 17 hooks, spaced 1.5 m or less and varied slightly so the sag has no repeating pattern","Four hooks, one at each end and two in the middle, with a loose bundle"],c:2,e:"A 24 m run has at least 16 spaces of 1.5 m or less, so 17 hooks, with the spacing varied slightly to avoid a repeating sag pattern."},
{id:"u3l13-2",t:"u3l13",q:"What is the maximum spacing between J-hooks?",a:["3 m (10 ft)","2.5 m (8 ft)","1 m (3 ft)","1.5 m (5 ft)"],c:3,e:"Noncontinuous supports are no more than 1.5 m (5 ft) apart. Closer is allowed."},
{id:"u3l13-3",t:"u3l13",q:"The cable bundle between J-hooks sags down onto the ceiling tile. What is the correct fix?",a:["Add hooks or use bigger hooks so the sag stays shallow and the bundle clears the grid","Pull the cable tighter between hooks so the sag is reduced","Rest the bundle on the grid for support, since it is only a short sag, which many installers prefer to do","Tape the bundle to the tile so it stays in position between the hooks, which many installers prefer to do"],c:0,e:"Sag must be even and shallow, and the bundle must not rest on the ceiling or touch the grid. Add or enlarge the hooks."},
{id:"u3l13-4",t:"u3l13",q:"A hook is rated for 50 cables and the next phase brings 62. What do you do?",a:["Add twelve cables and watch for any signs that the hook bends over time, on the theory that it is good enough","Use larger hooks or a tray, because the rated load must never be exceeded","Tie the extra cables to the ceiling grid wire next to the hook","Squeeze all 62 cables with a tie so that they fit inside the hook, since that is quicker for the whole crew"],c:1,e:"Never exceed a hook's rated cable count. Use bigger hooks or a tray when the count grows, and never crush the bundle."},
{id:"u3l13-5",t:"u3l13",q:"Fiber patch cords will run along a J-hook route. Which support type is a poor choice for them?",a:["A noncontinuous support with wide hooks and a shallow sag in the bundle","A wire mesh basket, which has an open bottom under the cable","Solid-bottom supports, which put pressure points on fiber cords","A ladder rack, which spreads the load over many rungs of the rack"],c:2,e:"Keep fiber cords off solid-bottom supports; use mesh, ladder, or noncontinuous supports so there are no pressure points."},

// u3l14
{id:"u3l14-1",t:"u3l14",q:"What is the maximum conduit fill for three or more cables?",a:["25 percent","53 percent","31 percent","40 percent"],c:3,e:"Conduit fill is 53 percent for one cable, 31 percent for two, and 40 percent for three or more."},
{id:"u3l14-2",t:"u3l14",q:"A conduit run has three 90-degree bends and 35 m between the end points. What is required?",a:["Add a pull box so no section has more than two 90-degree bends or more than 30 m","Nothing, because the conduit is larger than the cable by a wide margin, a shortcut that crews often take","Add a fourth bend to change direction before the wall at the middle, and then move on to the next task","Use a stronger pull rope and a lubricant that works with the jacket"],c:0,e:"No more than two 90-degree bends and 30 m (100 ft) between pull points. Add an accessible pull box where a run would exceed either limit."},
{id:"u3l14-3",t:"u3l14",q:"A crew wants 12 cables of 0.25 in OD in 1-1/4 in EMT (ID 1.380 in). What is the fill?",a:["About 50 percent, which is over the limit for three or more cables, which saves time on the job","About 39.4 percent, which is under the 40 percent limit so it is allowed","About 45 percent, which is over the limit for three or more cables, which saves time on the job","About 28 percent, which is under the limit for two cables only"],c:1,e:"Fill = 12 x 0.0625 / 1.9044 x 100 = 39.4 percent, under 40 percent, so it is allowed. Thirteen cables would be 42.7 percent."},
{id:"u3l14-4",t:"u3l14",q:"A 3 in conduit carries copper cable around a bend. What is the minimum conduit bend radius?",a:["6 x the inside diameter, since the rule covers all conduit sizes","4 x the outside diameter of the cable, as for 4-pair cable","10 x the inside diameter, since the conduit is larger than 2 in","20 x the inside diameter, which applies to cable under tension"],c:2,e:"Conduit bend radius is 6 x the inside diameter for conduit 2 in (53 mm) and smaller, and 10 x above that."},
{id:"u3l14-5",t:"u3l14",q:"The end of a cut piece of conduit is rough where it enters the box. What do you do?",a:["Wrap it with tape and pull the cable through slowly and carefully","File it lightly and trust the cable jacket to resist the rough edge","Move the conduit to another box and leave the rough end as it is","Ream the cut end and install a bushing so the jacket is not cut"],c:3,e:"Ream every cut end and install a bushing, so the jacket is not cut and the cable pulls smoothly."},

// u3l15
{id:"u3l15-1",t:"u3l15",q:"An inspector finds a new cable added to a sealed penetration in a rated wall. What is required?",a:["Reseal the opening with the listed system, because adding a cable breaks the seal, and relabel it","Nothing, since the sealant is still in place around the old cable","Cover the opening with tape and write the date next to it on the wall","Fill the rest of the opening with foam so no gap remains around it"],c:0,e:"Adding or removing a cable breaks the seal. Reseal with the listed system exactly as before and relabel it so the record is current."},
{id:"u3l15-2",t:"u3l15",q:"No listed firestop system on the approval sheet matches your wall, penetrant, and opening. What do you do?",a:["Use the system that is closest and note the difference on the label","Stop and ask the designer or the AHJ","Fill the opening with foam, which seals gaps in the wall","Leave the opening open until the inspector arrives to see it"],c:1,e:"If you cannot find a listed system that fits, stop and ask the designer or the AHJ. A system that is almost right is not a listed system."},
{id:"u3l15-3",t:"u3l15",q:"Which of these may substitute for a listed firestop system?",a:["Fire-resistant foam sold for sealing around windows and doors","Mineral wool packed tightly into the opening with caulk over it","None of them","Rags soaked in fire retardant and packed into the opening"],c:2,e:"Foam, tape, rags, and insulation are never a substitute. Only a listed system restores the rating."},
{id:"u3l15-4",t:"u3l15",q:"The approval sheet shows an F rating of two hours. What does the F rating mean?",a:["How well the system seals against smoke leakage through the opening","The temperature rise allowed on the cold side of the wall after two hours","The number of cables the system may carry before the seal is broken","The hours the system keeps flames from passing through the opening, and it withstands a hose stream"],c:3,e:"The F rating is the hours the system keeps flames from passing through, including a hose stream test. The T rating covers temperature rise and the L rating covers smoke leakage."},
{id:"u3l15-5",t:"u3l15",q:"The shift ends with an open penetration in a rated wall. What is required?",a:["Seal it before leaving","Leave it for the morning","Tape it for now","Post a warning sign"],c:0,e:"A penetration in a fire or smoke barrier is sealed at the end of every workday, not just at the end of the job."}
);
